\maketitle


\begin{abstract}
The paper compares mean–variance optimization with alternatives that constrain risk using MAD, IQR, and a wider central-quantile range for nine U.S. sector ETFs over 25 years, all under the same constraints: long-only, fully invested, and a maximum allocation cap with an explicit cash sleeve. The paper evaluates these risk metrics across various annualized risk budgets and rebalancing schedules under a strict no-look-ahead timeline. The main takeaway is that rebalancing frequencies dominate the risk proxy, so performance differences across rebalancing schedules are typically larger than differences across volatility/MAD/IQR/95\% once constraints are imposed. Moreover, the robust risk proxies are feasible but not a free lunch, as at longer rebalance intervals, they often produce similar allocations and realized outcomes in this setting.
\end{abstract}

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Keywords: } MAD and Quantile risk, Markowitz portfolio construction, Sector ETFs Portfolios

\section{Introduction}
Portfolio optimization is often presented through the mean--variance framework, but implementable versions of these problems rely on risk estimates computed from finite samples of daily returns. In financial data, a small number of tail observations can disproportionately influence classical volatility estimates, and therefore materially affect the risk constraints that drive portfolio weights. In practice, this sensitivity can lead to unstable allocations across rebalances, particularly when the estimation window is short or markets experience abrupt drawdowns.

This paper studies whether simple, more robust dispersion proxies change realized outcomes in a constrained, implementable setting. The paper compares volatility-constrained optimization against alternatives that constrain mean absolute deviation (MAD), the interquartile range (IQR), and a wider central-quantile range (2.5\%--97.5\%, labeled ``95\%''). These measures are attractive because they are interpretable and easy to compute on rolling windows, and because they target the dispersion of returns without allowing a handful of extreme observations to dominate the constraint by construction.

These proxies are not assumed to dominate variance-based volatility in all settings; rather, the question is whether they lead to meaningfully different allocations, turnover, and net performance once implementability constraints and estimation-window design are fixed.

Empirically, the analysis focuses on a liquid, interpretable universe of U.S. sector ETFs over a long sample (1999--2025), with long-only and fully invested constraints and a maximum weight cap to avoid unrealistic concentration. The analysis evaluates annualized risk budgets (5\%, 10\%, 15\%) and rebalancing schedules (1 month, 3 months, 6 months, and 1 year) under a strict no-look-ahead timeline. Because optimized strategies can trade frequently, the paper also reports a transaction-cost robustness analysis using proportional costs that scale with turnover.

\noindent\textbf{Motivating evidence: semi-annual outlier inflation in volatility.}
To illustrate why robust dispersion proxies are plausible substitutes for volatility in daily-return windows, the paper summarizes the distribution of daily sector-ETF returns by \emph{semi-annual} period and visualizes how volatility estimates differ when outliers are included versus removed using an IQR-based rule. The purpose is not to present an investable signal, but to highlight a stylized fact: \emph{standard deviation computed on all observations can substantially exceed a trimmed estimate during half-years with large tail events}.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includefigure[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/motivation_semiannual_outlier_inflation.png}
	\caption{Semi-annual distributions of daily simple returns for sector ETFs (pooled within each half-year). Boxes show $Q_1$--$Q_3$ with median; whiskers follow the $1.5 \times \text{IQR}$ rule; points denote outliers. The mean is marked, with overlaid $\pm 2\sigma$ bands computed on all daily returns (dashed orange) versus after removing IQR-defined outliers (dotted green).}
	\label{fig:motivation-outlier-inflation}
\end{figure}

\noindent\textbf{Practitioner takeaway.}
If a small number of extreme days can materially inflate dispersion estimates, dispersion-based constraints can become ``tighter'' than intended and induce larger reallocations. Robust dispersion proxies are one practical way to reduce the influence of these episodes within a daily risk constraint, but their value must ultimately be judged by net outcomes after turnover and costs.

This paper makes three contributions. First, it provides an apples-to-apples comparison of volatility-, MAD-, IQR-, and ``95\%''-constrained portfolio optimization under the same implementable constraints (long-only, fully invested, and a maximum allocation cap with an explicit cash sleeve). Second, it systematically evaluates these strategies across risk budgets and rebalancing schedules, isolating how the estimation horizon and rebalance frequency interact with both realized risk and turnover. Third, it emphasizes backtest integrity by enforcing a strict no-look-ahead timeline and by reporting outcomes both before and after proportional transaction costs, so that any apparent advantage of a risk proxy is judged on economic viability rather than on frictionless performance alone.

\section{Related Work and Background}
Modern portfolio selection begins with the mean--variance paradigm \parencite{Markowitz1952}, which formalizes the trade-off between expected return and variance, and the two-fund separation result in the presence of a risk-free asset \parencite{Tobin1958}. In practice, however, implementable optimization depends on noisy, time-varying estimates of inputs---especially expected returns and the covariance matrix--- \parencite{Merton1980,Chopra1993,Best1975}. A long-standing critique is that the optimizer can become an ``error maximizer,'' producing extreme allocations that are optimal for the estimated inputs but unstable out-of-sample \parencite{Michaud1989}.

Two themes recur in the literature on making optimized portfolios more stable. The first is to incorporate economically motivated constraints and regularization. Even when constraints are not ``structural truths,'' they can reduce the optimizer's sensitivity and improve realized risk outcomes; long-only and position limits are canonical examples \parencite{Jagannathan2003}. The second is to improve estimation. Shrinkage estimators can stabilize covariance matrices \parencite{Ledoit2003}, and Bayesian/shrinkage approaches to expected returns can reduce the influence of noisy sample means \parencite{Jorion1986}. A complementary perspective emphasizes that, in many realistic settings, simple diversified allocations can be difficult to beat out-of-sample, providing a useful benchmark for optimization-based strategies \parencite{DeMiguel2009}.

This contribution sits at the intersection of these themes: implementability constraints are kept fixed, and the paper studies whether swapping the risk proxy inside the constraint---from variance-based volatility to robust dispersion measures computed on daily returns---materially changes allocations, turnover, and net performance.

\subsection{Linear and robust alternatives to variance}
Variance penalizes squared deviations and is therefore sensitive to extreme observations. Because financial returns often display skewness, excess kurtosis, and tail events, alternative risk measures have been widely explored. Mean absolute deviation (MAD) replaces squared deviations with absolute deviations, yielding a linear programming formulation with attractive computational properties \parencite{Konno1991}. Subsequent work studies linear programming-based portfolio optimization more broadly, including robust and downside-oriented formulations \parencite{Mansini2014}. Empirically, comparisons between mean--variance and MAD often emphasize differences in sensitivity to estimation error and tail observations \parencite{Simaan1997}.

Beyond MAD, a large class of robust portfolio methods modifies either the loss/risk functional (to reduce tail sensitivity) or the input estimation (to reduce parameter uncertainty). Minimax-style and related robust formulations can often be expressed in linear or convex programs, offering transparent constraint-driven control of extreme outcomes \parencite{Young1998}. Quantile-based measures provide another route to robustness by focusing on central dispersion or tail behavior rather than squared deviations.

Quantile-based and tail-focused risk measures, such as value-at-risk and conditional value-at-risk (CVaR), provide another route to robustness by explicitly controlling tail losses; CVaR optimization is convex and has a well-known linear programming representation \parencite{Rockafellar2000}. Downside-risk performance measures, such as the Sortino ratio, similarly emphasize asymmetric risk \parencite{Sortino1994}. This paper focuses on dispersion proxies (MAD, IQR, and a wider central-quantile range labeled ``95\%'') that are simple to interpret and straightforward to compute on rolling windows; these measures are not intended to be the last word on tail risk, but rather a practical set of alternatives that can be embedded into a constrained, implementable optimizer.

\subsection{Turnover, rebalancing frequency, and transaction costs}
Rebalancing can improve risk control and allow the investor to respond to changing return and covariance structure, but it mechanically induces turnover. Turnover matters because trading is costly: even in liquid markets, the cost of active investing can materially erode performance \parencite{French2008}. Classic theory makes this point sharply: with transaction costs, optimal policies generally involve a trade-off between tracking the desired target and avoiding excessive trading, often generating ``no-trade'' regions and gradual adjustment rather than continuous re-optimization \parencite{Constantinides1986}.

From an implementation perspective, costs are not only explicit fees, but also spreads and market impact. The market microstructure literature formalizes how trading moves prices and how liquidity provision affects execution costs \parencite{Kyle1985}, while optimal execution models characterize the trade-off between impact and timing when trading a target position \parencite{Almgren2001}. More recent dynamic portfolio choice work emphasizes that predictable returns can justify trading, but that transaction costs, and risk constraints fundamentally shape optimal turnover \parencite{Garleanu2013}. In this sector-ETF setting, the paper uses a simple proportional cost model applied to turnover as a deliberately transparent proxy for these frictions, and evaluates whether risk-proxy differences translate into economically meaningful turnover differences.

\subsection{Backtest integrity}
Because optimization and rebalancing are sensitive to the information set, careful backtest design is essential. The backtest follows a strict no-look-ahead timeline: weights for a holding period are computed using only returns from the immediately preceding estimation window (\Cref{fig:no-lookahead}). This design choice ensures that the performance evaluation reflects an implementable process rather than inadvertent leakage of future information.

More broadly, the backtesting literature cautions that repeated searches over strategies, hyperparameters, and sample splits can lead to data snooping and selection bias, in which apparently strong in-sample results fail out of sample. A foundational reference is the ``reality check'' approach, which explicitly accounts for multiple testing when evaluating strategy performance \parencite{White2000}. In addition, practical guidance emphasizes guarding against backtest overfitting and selection bias when comparing many strategy variants \parencite{LopezdePrado2018}. While this paper does not attempt formal multiple-testing adjustments, it adopts a transparency-first approach (fixed rules, explicit timelines, and reported turnover/cost sensitivity) intended to reduce the scope for inadvertent overfitting.

\section{Data and Methodology}
\subsection{Data used and collected}
This paper studies a liquid and interpretable U.S. equity universe consisting of nine Select Sector SPDR ETFs:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item XLB (Materials)
    \item XLE (Energy)
    \item XLF (Financials)
    \item XLI (Industrials)
    \item XLK (Technology)
    \item XLP (Consumer Staples)
    \item XLU (Utilities)
    \item XLV (Health Care)
    \item XLY (Consumer Discretionary)
\end{enumerate}
As these 9 ETFs have been in existence for our entire test period, our portfolio is restricted to them. Portfolio weights are chosen over this nine-asset sector universe. As a benchmark, the S\&P~500 index (\texttt{\string^GSPC}) is used.

In addition, the optimizer is allowed to hold residual wealth in a cash-equivalent sleeve (CSH) with a daily return of 0. This makes cash an explicit asset in the feasible set but does not change the risky-asset universe.

Daily adjusted-close price data are collected from Yahoo Finance and merged into a single panel dataset. The sample in the current build runs from 1999--01--04 through 2025--12--30. Let $P_{t,i}$ denote the adjusted close price for asset $i$ on day $t$. The daily \emph{simple} return is defined as
\begin{equation}
	r_{t,i} \triangleq \frac{P_{t,i}}{P_{t-1,i}} - 1.
\end{equation}
Arithmetic returns are used throughout to match the backtest implementation.

\noindent\textbf{Data and return-definition note.}
Adjusted close prices reflect splits and distributions as provided by the vendor; results, therefore, inherit vendor conventions and any associated data-quality issues. The analysis does not model taxes, borrowing costs, or short rebates; cash is modeled as a zero-return sleeve.

\subsection{Returns and period construction}
Daily returns are aggregated into non-overlapping holding periods according to the chosen rebalancing schedule (1mo, 3mo, 6mo, 1y). For each holding period, portfolio performance is computed by applying the period's weights to daily returns and compounding within the period.

These non-overlapping periods are referred to as \emph{holding periods} (i.e., rebalance intervals).

\subsection{Risk measures}
All risk constraints are applied to dispersion computed on \emph{daily} portfolio returns within the estimation window $\{r_{p,t}\}_{t=1}^T$.

\subsubsection{Volatility (standard deviation)}
This paper uses the sample standard deviation of daily portfolio returns as the classical variance-based dispersion proxy,
\begin{equation}
	\sigma(r_p) \triangleq \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(r_p)}.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Mean absolute deviation (MAD)}
MAD replaces squared deviations with absolute deviations. For a return series $r_p$ with mean $\mathbb{E}[r_p]$, the population MAD is
\begin{equation}
	\mathrm{MAD}(r_p) \triangleq \mathbb{E}\left[\lvert r_p - \mathbb{E}[r_p] \rvert\right],
\end{equation}
with the obvious sample analog used in implementation \parencite{Konno1991}.

\subsubsection{Interquartile range (IQR)}
The IQR summarizes central dispersion via the difference between the 75th and 25th percentiles of daily returns,
\begin{equation}
	\mathrm{IQR}(r_p) \triangleq Q_3(r_p) - Q_1(r_p).
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Central 95\% range (``95\%'')}
As a wider central-dispersion proxy, this paper uses the central 95\% range between the 97.5th and 2.5th percentiles of daily returns (labeled ``95\%'' in figures and tables),
\begin{equation}
	\mathrm{CR95}(r_p) \triangleq Q_{0.975}(r_p) - Q_{0.025}(r_p).
\end{equation}

\subsection{Implementation details and constraints}
\label{sec:impl-details}
\subsubsection{Notation}
Let $\mathcal{S}$ denote the set of nine sector ETFs, and let CSH denote a cash-equivalent asset with daily return $r_{\mathrm{CSH},t}=0$.
The implementation uses an explicit $N=10$-asset return vector $r_t \in \mathbb{R}^N$ that stacks the nine sector-ETF returns and the cash return. A portfolio is characterized by weights $w \in \mathbb{R}^N$, producing daily portfolio returns $r_{p,t} = w^\top r_t$.

\subsubsection{Implementable constraints}
Portfolios are constrained to be long-only and fully invested across the sector ETFs and cash:
\begin{align}
	\sum_{i=1}^N w_i &= 1, \\
	w_i &\ge 0 \quad \forall i.
\end{align}
A maximum allocation constraint is imposed on \emph{sector ETFs only} to prevent extreme concentration:
\begin{equation}
	0 \le w_i \le w_{\max} \quad \forall i \in \mathcal{S},\qquad 0 \le w_{\mathrm{CSH}} \le 1.
\end{equation}
In the implementation used for the paper build, sector-ETF weights are capped at $w_{\max}=25\%$, and the residual is held in cash via the full-investment constraint.

\paragraph{Cash modeling assumption.}
The feasible set includes a cash-equivalent asset (CSH) with a daily return of 0 to allow the optimizer to de-risk by allocating to cash. This implies no earned interest on idle cash; cash is treated purely as a risk-reduction instrument. Consistent with this assumption, the risk-free rate is set to zero when reporting risk-adjusted ratios.

\subsubsection{Optimization objective and portfolio problems}
For each rebalance date, expected returns $\hat\mu$ are estimated as mean \emph{daily} returns over the immediately prior estimation window and weights are chosen to maximize $\hat\mu^\top w$ subject to a risk budget.

\paragraph{Solver.}
Constrained nonlinear optimization is solved using SciPy's SLSQP routine.

\subsection{Portfolio optimization problems}
For each rebalance date, weights are chosen to maximize estimated mean return subject to a dispersion budget and implementability constraints.

\paragraph{Volatility-constrained portfolio.}
\begin{align}
	\max_{w} \quad & \hat\mu^\top w \\
	\text{s.t.}\quad & \sqrt{w^\top \hat\Sigma w} \le \sigma_{\max}, \\
	& \sum_{i=1}^N w_i = 1,\; w_i \ge 0 \;\;\forall i,\; w_i \le w_{\max} \;\;\forall i\in\mathcal{S}.
\end{align}

\paragraph{MAD-constrained portfolio.}
Define the sample MAD of portfolio returns over the estimation window; then solve
\begin{align}
	\max_{w} \quad & \hat\mu^\top w \\
	\text{s.t.}\quad & \mathrm{MAD}(\{w^\top r_t\}) \le \mathrm{MAD}_{\max}, \\
	& \sum_{i=1}^N w_i = 1,\; w_i \ge 0 \;\;\forall i,\; w_i \le w_{\max} \;\;\forall i\in\mathcal{S}.
\end{align}

\paragraph{IQR- and central-quantile-range-constrained portfolios.}
Analogously constrain $\mathrm{IQR}(\{w^\top r_t\})$ (or a wider central quantile range) to a budget.

\subsection{Risk budget calibration across measures}
Risk budgets are parameterized at an annualized scale and mapped to measure-specific constraints, allowing different dispersion proxies to be compared under a common target risk level.

\paragraph{Annual-to-daily risk scaling (to ensure comparability across rebalancing schedules).}
All risk constraints are applied to risk computed on \emph{daily} portfolio returns within the estimation window. Let $\sigma_{\max}^{\mathrm{ann}}$ denote the annualized risk budget (5\%, 10\%, 15\%). This is converted into a daily target via the square-root-of-time rule,
\begin{equation}
	\sigma_{\max}^{\mathrm{day}} \triangleq \frac{\sigma_{\max}^{\mathrm{ann}}}{\sqrt{252}},
\end{equation}
which follows from the variance-additivity of (approximately) uncorrelated daily returns under standard modeling assumptions \parencite{Campbell1997,Hull2018}.
The optimizer is then constrained using risk functions computed in daily units. This design fixes the \emph{annualized} risk budget across rebalancing frequencies while still using only information available at each rebalance.

\paragraph{Measure-to-measure calibration (normal approximation).}
To place MAD and quantile-range constraints on a scale comparable to volatility, normal-approximation mappings are applied to daily returns. Under a normal model, these dispersion measures are fixed multiples of $\sigma$ and can be expressed in closed form using standard normal quantiles \parencite{CasellaBerger2002}:
\begin{align}
	\mathrm{MAD}_{\max}^{\mathrm{day}} &\triangleq \sigma_{\max}^{\mathrm{day}}\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{\pi}}, \\
	\mathrm{IQR}_{\max}^{\mathrm{day}} &\triangleq 2\,\sigma_{\max}^{\mathrm{day}}\,\Phi^{-1}(0.75) \approx 1.349\,\sigma_{\max}^{\mathrm{day}}, \\
	\mathrm{CR95}_{\max}^{\mathrm{day}} &\triangleq 2\,\sigma_{\max}^{\mathrm{day}}\,\Phi^{-1}(0.975) \approx 3.92\,\sigma_{\max}^{\mathrm{day}}.
\end{align}
These mappings are used only to define comparable \emph{constraint levels}; realized risk metrics are reported directly.

\paragraph{Annualization of reported dispersion measures.}
For reporting, realized dispersion measures computed on daily returns are annualized using the square-root-of-time rule (as for volatility). For example, $\mathrm{AnnMAD}=\sqrt{252}\,\mathrm{MAD}^{\mathrm{day}}$; the same scaling is applied to IQR and the central 95\% range.

\subsection{Backtest design}
\subsubsection{Rebalancing schedules}
Rebalancing schedules of 1 month, 3 months, 6 months, and 1 year are evaluated. Each schedule defines a sequence of non-overlapping holding periods.

\subsubsection{No-look-ahead timeline}
This study enforces a strict information timeline.
\begin{enumerate}
	\item At the start of holding period $k$, estimate $\hat\mu$ and risk inputs \emph{only} from daily returns in the immediately prior period $k-1$.
	\item Solve the optimization problem to obtain weights $w_k$.
	\item Apply $w_k$ to realized daily returns within period $k$ to compute performance.
\end{enumerate}
This design ensures that weights for period $k$ do not use any return information from within period $k$.

Because the estimation window is defined as the immediately prior holding period, the estimation window length equals the holding period length for each rebalancing schedule.

\paragraph{Rebalance-day mechanics.}
Weights $w_k$ become effective at the start of period $k$; proportional transaction costs (when modeled) are applied immediately before earning period-$k$ returns.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includefigure[width=0.60\linewidth]{figures/no_lookahead_timeline.png}
	\caption{Backtest timeline. Inputs for period $k$ weights are estimated using only period $k-1$ returns; weights are then held during period $k$.}
	\label{fig:no-lookahead}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Transaction costs and turnover}
This paper models proportional transaction costs that scale with turnover. Let $w_k$ denote weights at rebalance $k$; define \emph{charged turnover} (total traded notional in risky assets as a fraction of portfolio value) as
\begin{equation}
	\tau_k \triangleq \sum_{i\in \mathcal{S}} \lvert w_{k,i} - w_{k-1,i} \rvert,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{S}$ is the set of sector ETFs (excluding the cash sleeve CSH). This corresponds to charging a one-way fee per trade on risky assets while treating the cash leg as free: reallocations between cash and a risky asset incur a one-way fee, whereas reallocations between risky assets incur both a sell and a buy fee.

For a transaction cost rate $c$ in basis points (bps), a rebalance-time cost proportional to traded notional is applied. In the baseline robustness analysis, $c\in\{0,1,2\}$ bps is used (equivalently 0.00\%, 0.01\%, 0.02\% per unit traded). The rebalance haircut is
\begin{equation}
	\mathrm{Cost}_k \triangleq c\,\tau_k\,V_{k^-},
\end{equation}
where $V_{k^-}$ is portfolio value immediately before rebalancing.
\noindent When the cash sleeve is absent (or fixed at zero), buys and sells in risky assets match in aggregate, and the corresponding one-way risky-asset turnover is $\tfrac{1}{2}\tau_k$.

\subsection{Performance metrics}
Performance is evaluated using both level and risk-adjusted measures.

\subsubsection{Terminal wealth and portfolio value}
Let $V_t$ denote portfolio value; the terminal $V_T$ is reported along with time-series plots of $V_t$.

\subsubsection{Risk and risk-adjusted return}
Realized volatility, realized MAD, and realized IQR are reported. Risk-adjusted performance is summarized using an explicit return-to-volatility ratio:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{CAGR/AnnVol} \triangleq \frac{\mathrm{CAGR}}{\mathrm{AnnVol}},
\end{equation}
where CAGR is used as the numerator to align with long-horizon compounding in the backtest. This explicit label (CAGR/AnnVol) is used rather than the conventional Sharpe ratio, which is typically defined using mean excess returns.

\subsubsection{Drawdown}
Maximum drawdown (MDD) is computed within each holding period from the running peak-to-trough decline of the cumulative return path implied by daily portfolio returns during that period. Tables report the worst within-period drawdown over time for each configuration.

\subsubsection{Turnover}
Turnover levels by strategy and rebalance schedule are reported as a key driver of transaction cost sensitivity.

\section{Empirical Results: 0 bps Transaction Costs}
\label{sec:results-0bps}
This section presents the core results in a frictionless setting (0 bps proportional transaction costs), emphasizing how outcomes vary by risk budget, risk proxy, and rebalancing schedule.
The paper presents the main figures and summary table in-text; additional diagnostic tables/figures are generated by the build pipeline but are not all reproduced in the main PDF to keep the paper compact.

Because the backtest evaluates a grid of specifications (risk budgets, frequencies, and risk proxies), the main text discussion is treated as a robustness summary rather than an exercise in selecting a single ``best'' configuration.

\noindent\textbf{What to look for in a frictionless setting.}
Before introducing transaction costs, it is useful to separate two questions: (i) whether rebalancing frequency changes performance mechanically through estimation-window length and how often the optimizer is ``allowed'' to react, and (ii) whether swapping the dispersion proxy inside the risk constraint materially changes outcomes once implementability constraints (long-only, max-weight cap, and an explicit cash sleeve) are imposed.
The results below provide direct evidence on both dimensions and motivate why turnover will become the dominant channel once proportional costs are introduced.

\subsection{Summary of frictionless results}
\Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost} reports realized outcomes across all risk budgets, strategies, and rebalancing frequencies (sorted by risk, then frequency, then strategy), together with realized risk, drawdown, and turnover. Three patterns stand out.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Rebalancing frequency is first-order.} Across the optimized strategies, 6-month rebalancing often produces the strongest terminal wealth outcomes in this sample; monthly optimization performs materially worse in terminal wealth despite satisfying the same annualized risk-budget targets.
	\item \textbf{Risk proxy choice is usually second-order.} At longer rebalance intervals (6 months and 1 year), the four risk proxies often produce comparable realized volatility and turnover, with performance differences that are modest relative to the effect of rebalancing frequency.
	\item \textbf{Turnover is mechanically high for optimization.} Even at 0 bps, monthly optimization generates turnover near 1 per rebalance (sum of absolute \emph{non-cash} weight changes), which foreshadows substantial cost sensitivity once proportional frictions are introduced (\Cref{sec:tc-robustness}).
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Discussion.}
The first two patterns indicate that, in this constrained sector-ETF universe, the choice of rebalancing schedule can dominate the choice of dispersion proxy in realized economic outcomes. In particular, intermediate rebalance intervals appear to strike a balance between (i) having enough data in each estimation window to reduce noise and (ii) updating weights often enough to react to changing return and covariance structure.
The third pattern is the key mechanism for the cost analysis: frequent re-optimization generates frequent trading, so even small proportional costs can compound into meaningful drag.

To anchor magnitudes, at the 10\% risk budget, the IQR strategy reaches a terminal portfolio value of 657.18 under 6-month rebalancing versus 371.84 under monthly rebalancing (with the S\&P benchmark at 469.37; see \Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost,fig:heatmap-pv-zero-cost}). The corresponding turnover levels (reported in \Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost}) are substantially higher under monthly rebalancing.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includefigure[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/pv_curves_zero_cost.png}
	\caption{Gross portfolio value paths (0 bps transaction costs) at a 10\% annualized risk budget. Panels correspond to rebalancing schedules (1mo, 3mo, 6mo, 1y); each panel compares strategies on the same dollar scale.}
	\label{fig:pv-zero-cost}
\end{figure}

\noindent\textbf{Practitioner takeaway.}
Even before costs, the rebalance interval changes the effective signal-to-noise ratio of inputs (because the estimation window equals the holding period) and can dominate which strategy ``wins'' in terminal wealth.

\subsection{Terminal wealth}
\Cref{fig:heatmap-pv-zero-cost} summarizes terminal wealth across all risk budgets and rebalancing schedules for each strategy.

\noindent\textbf{Relative performance and patterns.}
Two patterns stand out in \Cref{fig:heatmap-pv-zero-cost} and \Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost}. First, the dominant axis is \emph{rebalancing frequency}: for the optimized strategies, the 6-month schedule is often associated with the largest terminal wealth across risk budgets in this sample, though there are exceptions (e.g., some annual configurations under the ``95\%'' proxy). Monthly and quarterly optimization tend to underperform, even though they satisfy the same annualized risk-budget targets.
Second, differences across dispersion proxies are present but typically smaller than the rebalance-interval effect. Within a fixed rebalance interval, the IQR-constrained strategy often delivers the highest terminal wealth among the optimized variants (especially at 10\% risk), while volatility and MAD are frequently close to one another. The ``95\%'' range proxy is sometimes competitive at the annual rebalance horizon, suggesting that proxy choice can matter most when the optimizer is asked to make infrequent, larger allocation decisions.

\noindent\textbf{Benchmark comparison.}
The S\&P buy-and-hold benchmark provides a useful reality check: it is difficult for short-interval optimization (1mo/3mo) to overcome the combination of estimation noise and constrained implementation in this sector-ETF universe. Outperformance relative to the benchmark appears primarily in the 6-month configurations (and, more narrowly, in some annual configurations), reinforcing the practical idea that optimization benefits from longer windows and less frequent re-trading.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includefigure[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/heatmap_terminal_pv_zero_cost.png}
	\caption{Terminal gross portfolio value heatmap (0 bps transaction costs). Each panel shows one strategy; axes represent annualized risk budgets (vertical) and rebalancing schedules (horizontal). Cell values indicate the terminal portfolio value in dollars.}
	\label{fig:heatmap-pv-zero-cost}
\end{figure}

\noindent\textbf{Practitioner takeaway.}
If you are choosing between ``risk proxies'' inside a constrained optimizer, the heatmap suggests you should first choose a rebalance interval that is implementable and cost-aware; proxy choice is often a second-order tuning decision in this universe.

\subsection{Drawdowns and downside risk}
\Cref{fig:heatmap-mdd-zero-cost} summarizes worst within-period maximum drawdowns.

\noindent\textbf{Relative performance and patterns.}
Drawdowns are driven primarily by the market regime and the effective risk exposure implied by the risk budget, but the heatmap indicates a consistent pattern: downside outcomes vary across rebalance intervals even when the risk budget is held fixed (\Cref{fig:heatmap-mdd-zero-cost}). In particular, the worst drawdowns tend to be materially larger at the quarterly-to-annual horizons than at the monthly horizon. This is consistent with the fact that (i) longer holding periods can experience deeper peak-to-trough moves before the next rebalance resets exposures and (ii) drawdowns are measured within each holding period and then summarized as the worst such drawdown over time.

Across dispersion proxies, drawdown differences are generally modest relative to the rebalance-interval effect. This indicates that, within this constrained implementation (long-only, max-weight cap, and an explicit cash sleeve), swapping volatility for MAD/IQR/central-quantile dispersion does not systematically \emph{reduce} tail losses in a way that dominates the effect of how frequently the portfolio is allowed to update.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includefigure[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/heatmap_mdd_zero_cost.png}
	\caption{Maximum drawdown heatmap at 0 bps transaction costs. Each panel shows one strategy; axes represent risk budgets (vertical) and rebalancing schedules (horizontal). Cell values indicate maximum drawdown \emph{magnitude} in percent (higher values indicate larger drawdowns).}
	\label{fig:heatmap-mdd-zero-cost}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Realized risk and risk-adjusted performance}
To keep the main text streamlined, it focuses on realized annualized volatility and a return-to-volatility ratio (CAGR/AnnVol, with $r_f=0$), both reported alongside performance in \Cref{tab:summary-zero-cost}. Alternative proxy-specific return-to-risk ratios and related diagnostic grids are omitted for brevity.

\noindent\textbf{Relative performance and patterns.}
Two consistency checks are useful when interpreting the performance grids. First, realized dispersion generally increases with the stated risk budget across all strategies, indicating that the annual-to-daily scaling and proxy-to-proxy calibration produce broadly sensible monotonic behavior in realized risk. Second, across the four optimized strategies, realized risk levels are often quite similar within a (risk, frequency) cell, as expected given that all optimizers face the same implementation constraints and target dispersion budgets on the same underlying daily-return data.

In terms of risk-adjusted performance, differences across proxies are present but not uniform across rebalance intervals. The configurations with the highest terminal wealth are not simply those with the highest realized volatility; rather, they tend to correspond to combinations where the optimizer captures higher compounded returns while keeping realized dispersion near the target. This reinforces the paper's central theme: in a constrained, implementable setting, the \emph{interaction} between estimation window length and rebalance frequency can dominate the incremental choice of dispersion proxy.

\input{tables/summary_zero_cost}

\noindent The results above summarize \emph{performance} at 0 bps. The discussion next turns to the \emph{portfolio mechanics} behind these outcomes---weight paths and turnover---because these will be the primary channel through which proportional transaction costs affect net performance (\Cref{sec:weights-turnover-0bps, sec:tc-robustness}).

\section{Portfolio Weights and Turnover (0 bps)}
\label{sec:weights-turnover-0bps}
This section focuses on weight distributions and turnover across rebalancing frequencies and strategies.
These allocation dynamics provide the foundation for the transaction cost analysis in \Cref{sec:tc-robustness}.

\noindent\textbf{Evidence and interpretation.}
If proportional transaction costs scale with turnover, then weight dynamics are not merely descriptive: they determine the economic viability of a strategy. This section documents two empirically visible mechanisms.
First, turnover falls mechanically as the rebalancing interval increases, because weights are adjusted less frequently. Second, differences across risk proxies tend to appear through cash usage and concentration patterns rather than through completely different sector exposures.

Beyond these headlines, two additional points are important for interpretation. First, ``low turnover'' does not necessarily mean ``small trades'': lower-frequency portfolios often exhibit \emph{lumpier} reallocations (larger step changes at each rebalance) even if average turnover per rebalance is lower. Second, because estimation windows are defined as the immediately prior holding period, higher-frequency portfolios operate with shorter samples and can therefore exhibit more parameter noise, which mechanically increases trading as inputs fluctuate.

\subsection{Turnover across frequencies and strategies}
\Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost} reports turnover (mean rebalance-to-rebalance sum of absolute weight changes) across risk budgets and rebalancing schedules at 0 bps. The 10\% risk budget is highlighted as a representative ``medium'' case in the discussion below.

Turnover is computed as the rebalance-to-rebalance sum of absolute \emph{non-cash} weight changes (i.e., $\tau_k=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}} |w_{k,i}-w_{k-1,i}|$), consistent with the transaction-cost model in \Cref{sec:tc-robustness}. This treats the cash sleeve as a funding source/sink with no direct trading fee.

\noindent\textbf{Discussion.}
The monotone decline in turnover from 1-month to 1-year rebalancing provides a direct mechanism for why higher-frequency strategies can lag once proportional costs are introduced: the same cost rate is applied to a larger cumulative traded notional when turnover is persistently high (\Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost,fig:cost-drag,tab:tc-summary}).

\noindent Two patterns in \Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost} are especially relevant for the cost analysis.
First, \emph{frequency dominates}: even when turnover per rebalance is only modestly higher at 1-month than at 1-year, there are many more trading dates. A simple back-of-the-envelope annualized ``turnover intensity'' is $m\,\bar{\tau}$, where $m\in\{12,4,2,1\}$ is the number of rebalances per year and $\bar{\tau}$ is mean turnover per rebalance. At 10\% risk, the volatility strategy has $\bar{\tau}\approx 0.857$ at monthly rebalancing but $\bar{\tau}\approx 0.507$ at annual rebalancing; this implies roughly $12\times 0.857\approx 10.3$ turnover units per year versus $1\times 0.507\approx 0.5$ turnover units per year. Under a 1 bps proportional cost, that translates into an order-of-magnitude difference in expected annual cost drag (before compounding).

Second, \emph{risk budget matters more at short rebalance intervals}. At monthly rebalancing, allowing higher risk budgets leads to noticeably higher turnover (e.g., volatility: 0.587 at 5\% vs 0.857 at 10\% vs 0.956 at 15\%). At annual rebalancing, the same cross-risk spread is smaller (e.g., volatility: 0.392 at 5\% vs 0.507 at 10\% vs 0.649 at 15\%), suggesting that infrequent trading naturally limits how aggressively the optimizer can reposition.

Across proxies, turnover levels are broadly similar within a given (risk, frequency) cell. This helps explain why the transaction-cost penalty in \Cref{sec:tc-robustness} is comparable across volatility/MAD/IQR/``95\%'' in this implementation: swapping dispersion proxies changes the risk constraint but, by itself, does not deliver a systematic turnover advantage.

\input{tables/turnover_by_risk_zero_cost}

\noindent\textbf{Practitioner takeaway.}
Turnover is not just a diagnostic — it is an input to net performance. A strategy that looks competitive at 0 bps can become unattractive once turnover is multiplied by a realistic trading-cost assumption.

\subsection{Weight distributions through time (10\% risk budget)}
To isolate how the choice of dispersion proxy affects portfolio composition at a fixed risk budget, \Cref{fig:weights-area-strategy-10} plots sector and cash weights over time for each strategy at a 10\% risk budget (0 bps, monthly rebalancing).

\noindent\textbf{Discussion.}
Across strategies, the implementability constraints and the presence of a cash sleeve visibly limit extreme concentration, so the most salient differences tend to arise in (i) how aggressively the optimizer uses cash to satisfy the risk budget and (ii) how concentrated the sector mix becomes during particular regimes.
In economic terms, the cash sleeve acts as the optimizer's ``risk dial'': when the estimated dispersion of risky assets rises, the optimizer can satisfy the risk budget by shifting weight into cash rather than by forcing extreme sector shorts (which are disallowed). This generates a natural interpretation of proxy differences.
If a proxy treats a given episode as ``riskier'' (because of tail observations or a wider dispersion estimate), the optimizer tends to lean more heavily on cash and reduces active sector tilts; if a proxy is less sensitive, it tends to remain more fully invested and expresses risk through a larger risky allocation.

The figures also emphasize a practical point about implementability: the portfolios rotate across sectors over time, but the rotations are smooth and bounded by the cap constraint. As a result, much of the cross-strategy variation is in \emph{how much} active risk is taken (cash usage and concentration) rather than \emph{which} sectors are held.

