\subsection{Rebalance-interval allocation dynamics (stacked weight areas)}
To visualize how rebalancing frequency shapes the allocation path directly, the paper plots stacked area charts of \emph{sector plus cash} weights through time (each panel corresponds to a rebalance interval; areas sum to 100\%).

\noindent\textbf{Discussion.}
The stacked-area panels illustrate that longer rebalance intervals tend to produce smoother weight paths between rebalances, but also more discrete step changes at rebalance dates. This matters for costs because proportional turnover models effectively charge for \emph{total traded notional} rather than for ``smoothness'' per se.

Two economic interpretations follow. First, higher-frequency strategies are exposed to \emph{estimation noise} because they recalibrate on shorter samples, which can create churn even when the true opportunity set is stable. Second, lower-frequency strategies amortize estimation error over longer holds: they trade less often, but each trade can be larger because more drift accumulates between rebalances. In this sample, the rebalancing count appears to be the dominant driver of the gap in cumulative turnover intensity and therefore cost drag (\Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost,fig:cost-drag}).

Taken together, the weight-path figures provide a visual bridge between the frictionless performance section and the transaction cost section: turnover and weight instability are the conduits through which costs impact terminal wealth, and they are primarily controlled by the rebalance schedule rather than the choice among closely related dispersion proxies.

