\section{Robustness: Transaction Costs}
\label{sec:tc-robustness}
This section tests whether the frictionless ranking persists under proportional transaction costs that penalize turnover.
Because proportional costs are driven by trading volume, the turnover patterns documented in \Cref{sec:weights-turnover-0bps} provide the mechanism linking rebalancing frequency to net performance.

The transaction cost model used here is deliberately simple and transparent: a proportional haircut applied to turnover at each rebalance. This stylized structure isolates the economic channel (turnover) while abstracting from implementation details such as time-varying spreads, nonlinear market impact, and optimal execution. The section concludes with a discussion of how results would change under more refined cost structures.

\subsection{Economic viability under proportional costs}
\Cref{fig:cost-drag,tab:tc-summary} evaluate net terminal wealth under transaction cost rates of 0, 1, and 2 bps applied proportionally to turnover. These levels are intended to represent a low-friction ETF implementation environment (spreads/fees/slippage aggregated into a simple proportional cost) while still highlighting the mechanical link between turnover and cost drag.

\paragraph{Turnover measurement and one-way vs two-way costs.}
Recall from \Cref{sec:impl-details} that turnover is measured as the sum of absolute weight changes in \emph{risky assets} (sector ETFs), treating the cash sleeve as a costless funding source:
\begin{equation*}
	\tau_k = \sum_{i\in \mathcal{S}} \lvert w_{k,i} - w_{k-1,i} \rvert.
\end{equation*}
This corresponds to charging a one-way cost on risky-asset trades: if the portfolio moves \$1 from cash into a sector ETF, it incurs a cost proportional to \$1; if it simultaneously moves \$1 out of another sector ETF into cash, that also incurs a cost proportional to \$1. The total charged turnover for such a rebalance is \$2 (one-way cost on each risky leg), not \$1.

In a setting without cash (or where cash is fixed at zero), the long-only and fully-invested constraints imply that buys and sells in risky assets match in aggregate, so the traditional ``one-way turnover'' is $\tfrac{1}{2}\tau_k$. With an active cash sleeve, however, net flows into or out of risky assets need not sum to zero at each rebalance, and $\tau_k$ directly reflects the total notional traded in risky assets.

The proportional haircut at rebalance $k$ is
\begin{equation*}
	\mathrm{Cost}_k = c\,\tau_k\,V_{k^-},
\end{equation*}
where $c\in\{0,1,2\}$ bps is the cost rate and $V_{k^-}$ is portfolio value immediately before rebalancing. Holding-period returns are then earned on the post-cost value $V_k = V_{k^-} - \mathrm{Cost}_k$.

\paragraph{Interpretation and magnitudes.}
A 1 bps proportional cost is intentionally conservative: liquid sector ETFs typically trade with bid-ask spreads well below 1 bps during normal market conditions. However, realized execution costs include not only the quoted spread but also timing costs, potential adverse selection when trading large sizes, and any slippage relative to the rebalance signal. The 1--2 bps range used here is therefore best interpreted as an ``all-in'' execution cost that aggregates these frictions into a single proportional rate.

The dominant driver of performance under costs is turnover. Strategies that rebalance more frequently and generate larger weight changes incur larger cumulative trading haircuts. In contrast, the buy-and-hold benchmark is comparatively insensitive because turnover is near zero. At lower rebalancing frequencies (6 months and 1 year), optimized strategies generally remain viable, but the ranking can still shift once costs are applied because small differences in turnover compound over long horizons.

\noindent For ease of comparison, the benchmark's net terminal value is invariant to the transaction-cost rate (it does not trade) and is therefore repeated across the frequency panels in \Cref{tab:tc-summary}.

Cost drag is smaller at 6-month/1-year horizons because (i) there are fewer rebalances and (ii) turnover per rebalance is typically lower, so cumulative traded notional is smaller. To anchor magnitudes, consider the back-of-the-envelope calculation from \Cref{sec:weights-turnover-0bps}: at a 10\% risk budget, monthly volatility optimization has annualized turnover intensity near $12 \times 0.857 \approx 10.3$, implying roughly 10 bps of cost drag per year under a 1 bps rate (before compounding). Over a multi-decade horizon, this compounds into a material wealth difference.

Because turnover levels are similar across the volatility-, MAD-, IQR-, and ``95\%''-constrained optimizations in this implementation, the transaction-cost penalty is also similar across these risk proxies. This is an important negative result: even if a robust dispersion proxy changes the notion of ``risk,'' it does not automatically deliver a turnover advantage.

\subsection{Alternative cost structures and extensions}
The proportional cost model used in this analysis is a first-order approximation. Several extensions would provide a more realistic representation of trading frictions, though at the cost of additional complexity.

\paragraph{Nonlinear market impact.}
A well-established result in market microstructure is that price impact grows nonlinearly with trade size \parencite{Almgren2001,Kyle1985}. In a simplified permanent-impact model, executing a trade of size $Q$ in an asset with daily volume $V$ incurs an impact proportional to $(Q/V)^\alpha$ for some $\alpha \in (0.5, 1)$, often calibrated near 0.5--0.6 for equities. For the liquid sector ETFs in this study, impact is likely negligible at modest rebalance sizes, but large reallocations (e.g., moving 10--20\% of portfolio value into a single ETF in a single day) could incur measurable impact.

If impact were modeled, the main qualitative result---that turnover drives cost drag---would persist, but the penalty for high-turnover strategies would be amplified because large discrete trades (which occur more frequently at short rebalance intervals) would be disproportionately expensive. This would further favor lower-frequency rebalancing.

\paragraph{Time-varying spreads and volatility-dependent costs.}
Spreads widen during stress periods and contract during calm markets. A more refined model could make $c$ time-varying, linked to measures of market liquidity or realized volatility. This would penalize rebalancing during crises (when dispersion estimates are noisiest and spreads are widest) and could interact with the risk-proxy choice: if robust proxies reduce the frequency of ``panic reallocations,'' they might deliver a secondary benefit through lower realized execution costs.

\paragraph{Optimal execution and temporary impact.}
The current model assumes trades execute instantaneously at a fixed cost. In practice, large orders are split over time to minimize the temporary impact, creating a trade-off between urgency and cost \parencite{Almgren2001}. Incorporating optimal execution would require specifying a within-rebalance trading horizon (e.g., trade over 1--2 days after the signal), which would smooth the realized cost but also introduce timing risk.

\paragraph{Turnover penalties and no-trade regions.}
A direct way to mitigate turnover is to add an explicit turnover penalty to the objective or to impose no-trade bands that prevent rebalancing unless weight drift exceeds a threshold. These approaches are standard in practice and can materially reduce trading intensity \parencite{Garleanu2013}. The current analysis does not implement such mechanisms, but the turnover diagnostics in \Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost} provide a baseline against which turnover-penalized variants could be compared.

\paragraph{Implications for robust proxies.}
If nonlinear impact or time-varying costs were introduced, the relative ranking of risk proxies could shift if one proxy systematically avoids large trades during high-cost regimes. However, given that turnover levels are already similar across proxies in this implementation (\Cref{tab:turnover-by-risk-zero-cost}), any such benefit would likely be second-order relative to the direct effect of rebalancing frequency.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includefigure[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/cost_drag_curves.png}
	\caption{Transaction cost drag at a 10\% annualized risk budget. Terminal net portfolio value plotted against proportional transaction cost rate (0, 1, 2 bps) for each strategy; panels correspond to rebalancing schedules (1mo, 3mo, 6mo, 1y).}
	\label{fig:cost-drag}
\end{figure}

\noindent\textbf{Practitioner takeaway.}
In this implementation, the cost curve is largely a turnover curve: reducing rebalance frequency (and/or explicitly penalizing turnover) is a more direct lever for improving net outcomes than swapping among closely related dispersion measures.

\input{tables/tc_summary_10pct_risk}

\section{Discussion}
This study highlights a practical hierarchy for portfolio construction in a constrained, implementable optimizer. The empirical results are best interpreted not as a ``horse race'' among dispersion formulas, but as evidence about which 
\emph{design levers} matter most in a realistic workflow that repeatedly (i) estimates inputs from finite windows, (ii) solves a constrained optimization problem, and (iii) trades to targets under costs.

Three high-level takeaways summarize the discussion.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Constraints and estimation windows are first-order.} Long-only, fully-invested portfolios with position limits can materially stabilize optimization \parencite{Jagannathan2003}. In this setting, the estimation-window design (here: ``previous holding period'') and rebalance frequency jointly determine how noisy inputs are and how often the optimizer is allowed to react, and these effects can dominate incremental changes in the dispersion proxy.
	\item \textbf{Risk-proxy mappings are useful but imperfect.} Calibrating MAD/IQR/CR95 budgets to an annualized ``risk target'' relies on distributional approximations (here: normal multiples). When returns are non-normal or time-varying, different proxies can bind in different regimes, so cross-proxy comparisons should be read as comparisons of \emph{implementations} rather than as statements of dominance of one risk concept over another.
	\item \textbf{Turnover is the primary channel for costs.} The transaction-cost robustness analysis reinforces the classic point that active policies must be evaluated net of trading frictions \parencite{French2008}. In these sector-ETF optimizations, frequent re-optimization mechanically increases traded notional, and even 1--2 bps can compound into economically meaningful drag.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Why proxy differences compress under constraints}
In unconstrained settings, volatility and robust dispersion measures can imply meaningfully different optimal solutions because the constraint set can ``tilt'' the optimizer away from tail-sensitive estimates. In this paper's constrained setting, several mechanisms compress those differences.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{The cash sleeve acts as a shared risk valve.} Because cash is available and earns a deterministic 0 return, every proxy can satisfy its risk budget by adjusting the \emph{risky share} (sector ETFs) versus cash. If one proxy estimates an episode as riskier, the optimizer can respond by increasing cash rather than by producing extreme offsetting sector positions (which are disallowed). This induces a common pattern: proxy differences show up primarily in cash usage and concentration intensity.
	\item \textbf{The max-weight cap prevents ``error-maximizing'' concentration.} A cap such as $w_{\max}=25\%$ limits the optimizer's ability to express small input differences via extreme positions. As a result, distinct dispersion constraints can lead to similar feasible corner solutions, especially at longer horizons where input estimates are less noisy.
	\item \textbf{The objective is mean return with noisy estimates.} Because $\hat\mu$ is estimated from short, rolling daily windows (equal to the holding period), the optimization is often driven by estimation noise in $\hat\mu$ subject to a binding risk constraint. Under this structure, changing the risk proxy can shift which constraint binds and how much cash is used, but it does not necessarily create a persistent ``alpha'' mechanism.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Design choices vs. empirical findings}
Two experimental design choices drive much of the cross-frequency behavior.
First, the estimation window equals the immediately prior holding period, so higher-frequency strategies use shorter samples to estimate inputs. This makes both $\hat\mu$ and dispersion estimates more variable, increasing the probability of frequent reallocations.
Second, identical implementability constraints apply to all proxies (long-only, full investment, a sector-only cap, and an explicit cash sleeve), which intentionally isolates the effect of changing the dispersion proxy but also tends to push solutions toward similar feasible allocations.

An implication is that the paper's ``rebalance frequency dominates'' finding should be read as a joint statement about (i) rebalance frequency and (ii) the specific information set induced by the estimation window. Alternative window choices (e.g., a fixed-length trailing window independent of holding period) could change the frequency comparison by decoupling ``how often you trade'' from ``how much data you estimate with.''

\subsection{Regime dependence and economic interpretation}
While the paper does not run a formal subperiod attribution, the largest shifts in allocations and turnover occur in well-known stress episodes (e.g., the early-2000s drawdown, the 2008--09 crisis, and 2020). In such regimes, robust proxies can dampen the influence of a handful of extreme daily observations inside the dispersion constraint, which tends to translate into higher cash allocations and (in some cases) less aggressive sector rotation.

However, in this constrained sector-ETF setting, the dominant driver of net outcomes remains the rebalance schedule and the turnover it induces. Robust dispersion proxies therefore function primarily as \emph{implementation tools}---a way to reduce tail sensitivity of the risk constraint---rather than as a standalone source of persistent outperformance.

\subsection{Practical implementation guidance}
For a practitioner deciding how to operationalize a constrained optimizer of this type, the results suggest a pragmatic sequence.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Pick a rebalance policy that is cost-aware first.} Start with a frequency that is operationally feasible and consistent with expected trading frictions; use turnover and a cost model as primary diagnostics.
	\item \textbf{Choose an estimation window intentionally.} If the goal is stability, consider decoupling the window length from the holding period (e.g., using a longer trailing window but trading less frequently).
	\item \textbf{Then tune the risk proxy.} MAD/IQR/CR95 can be valuable if risk budgets are intended to be robust to rare shocks, but proxy choice is unlikely to rescue an otherwise high-churn process.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations and robustness checks}
There are several limitations worth emphasizing, both to frame the conclusions appropriately and to clarify which extensions are most likely to change the results.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Universe and scope.} Results are for a narrow and interpretable sector-ETF universe under long-only, fully-invested constraints with a sector-only cap and a cash sleeve; findings may not generalize to broader universes, leverage, shorting, or factor portfolios.
	\item \textbf{Expected return estimation.} Optimized portfolios are sensitive to expected return estimates \parencite{Merton1980}. Using a raw rolling mean of daily returns is intentionally transparent but noisy; shrinkage/regularized mean estimators, Bayesian approaches, or signal-combination frameworks could reduce churn and potentially change the relative performance of rebalance frequencies.
	\item \textbf{Covariance and dependence structure.} The volatility strategy uses a sample covariance estimator in each window; results may differ under shrinkage covariance estimators or regime-dependent models \parencite{Ledoit2003}. Likewise, the square-root-of-time scaling assumes weak dependence; volatility clustering and serial correlation can cause realized annualization to deviate from $\sqrt{252}$ scaling.
	\item \textbf{Risk-budget calibration across measures.} The proxy-to-proxy mapping uses normal multipliers to set comparable constraint levels. This is a pragmatic calibration, not a structural truth; when distributions exhibit skewness/kurtosis, the mapping can cause one proxy to bind more tightly than another in specific regimes, affecting cash usage and turnover.
	\item \textbf{Transaction cost model.} Proportional costs are a stylized proxy for spreads and frictions. A more refined model could incorporate time-varying spreads, volatility-dependent execution costs, and market impact that grows nonlinearly with traded notional \parencite{Kyle1985,Almgren2001}. The current model is best viewed as a sensitivity analysis that isolates the role of turnover.
	\item \textbf{Cash yield and taxes.} The cash sleeve earns 0, and the analysis ignores taxes. In practice, cash yields can materially affect the attractiveness of de-risking, and after-tax turnover can amplify the cost disadvantage of high-frequency trading.
	\item \textbf{Robustness to alternative timelines.} The strict no-look-ahead design is a strength, but other implementable variants exist (e.g., overlapping estimation windows, delayed execution, or using end-of-day signals with next-day implementation). These can affect both turnover and realized performance.
	\item \textbf{Additional robustness checks (natural extensions).} Two particularly relevant checks are (i) penalizing turnover explicitly in the objective (or adding trading bands/no-trade regions) to test whether the cost sensitivity can be mitigated mechanically, and (ii) varying the cap $w_{\max}$ and the presence/return of cash to quantify how much of the proxy similarity is driven by feasible-set design.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Economic vs. statistical significance}
The paper primarily focuses on \emph{economic} significance: whether a strategy's edge survives implementability constraints and trading frictions, and whether differences are large enough to matter in terminal wealth, drawdowns, realized risk, and turnover.
This emphasis is deliberate because small changes in compounded wealth over multi-decade samples can be economically meaningful even when conventional statistical tests are underpowered.

Statistical significance is more difficult in portfolio backtests because returns are time-dependent and the effective sample size can be small, especially at low rebalancing frequencies where there are few non-overlapping holding periods. In addition, repeated evaluation over a grid of configurations (risk budgets, frequencies, proxies) introduces a multiple-comparisons/data-snooping concern \parencite{White2000,LopezdePrado2018}.

A publication-grade extension would therefore report uncertainty using block-resampling methods over holding periods (or other dependence-aware resampling) and would test differences in risk-adjusted performance using measures aligned to the stated objective. For example, one could report tests on differences in Sharpe-like ratios defined on mean excess returns \parencite{Jobson1981} (while acknowledging that the paper's main ratio uses CAGR/AnnVol for interpretability over long horizons). More generally, the correct inference target depends on the investor's utility and horizon: what matters is not whether one proxy ``wins'' a t-test, but whether the improvement is stable, implementable, and robust to plausible perturbations in estimation and costs.

\subsection{Hypothesis evaluation}
The evidence across the 0 bps results (\Cref{sec:results-0bps}), the weight/turnover diagnostics (\Cref{sec:weights-turnover-0bps}), and the transaction-cost robustness analysis (\Cref{sec:tc-robustness}) supports a unified empirical takeaway.

\noindent\textbf{Hypothesis 1 (frequency dominates).}
Rebalancing frequency and the information set implied by the estimation-window design are first-order determinants of outcomes. Intermediate rebalance intervals (notably 6 months in this sample) \emph{often} deliver stronger frictionless terminal wealth than fully invested, with a sector cap and an explicit cash sleeve, which reduces estimation noise, and less frequent trading reduces churn.

\noindent\textbf{Hypothesis 2 (proxy choice is second-order under implementability constraints).}
Once long-only, full investment, a sector cap, and an explicit cash sleeve are imposed, changing the dispersion proxy typically affects allocations through cash usage and mild concentration shifts rather than through radically different sector exposures. As a result, across many (risk, frequency) cells, realized outcomes are broadly similar across volatility/MAD/IQR/CR95, especially at longer horizons.

\noindent\textbf{Hypothesis 3 (turnover explains cost sensitivity).}
Because the cost model is proportional to turnover, net performance under costs is largely explained by turnover intensity. Higher-frequency strategies exhibit persistently larger cumulative traded notional, which explains why even low single-digit basis-point costs can materially change net rankings.

For exposition, the 10\% risk budget is treated as a representative ``medium'' case when illustrating allocation paths and cost drag, while retaining the full (risk, frequency, strategy) grid in the summary and turnover tables.

\section{Conclusion}
This paper evaluates volatility-, MAD-, IQR-, and central-quantile-range-constrained portfolio optimization in a constrained, implementable sector-ETF setting over a long historical sample and under a strict no-look-ahead timeline. The analysis emphasizes the interaction between (i) how risk is measured in daily windows, (ii) how often the portfolio is re-optimized, and (iii) how turnover translates into net performance under costs.

The core message for practitioners is a hierarchy of decisions.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Choose a rebalance interval and estimation window jointly.} In this universe and timeline (estimation window equals the holding period), differences across 1mo/3mo/6mo/1y schedules are typically larger than differences across dispersion proxies. More broadly, the window length determines input noise; the rebalance frequency determines how often that noise is ``traded.''
	\item \textbf{Treat turnover as a primary risk alongside drawdown and volatility.} When proportional costs are introduced---even at 1--2 bps---net outcomes are largely explained by turnover intensity. A configuration that looks attractive at 0 bps can lose its edge once repeated trading haircuts compound.
	\item \textbf{Use robust dispersion proxies as implementation choices, not silver bullets.} MAD/IQR/CR95 constraints are feasible and interpretable, but in this constrained ETF setting, they do not reliably deliver a systematic turnover advantage relative to volatility constraints. Any incremental benefit is most likely to come from (i) better expected-return estimation, (ii) explicit turnover-aware design (penalties, bands, or no-trade regions), or (iii) a different objective that directly trades off expected return against both risk and trading costs.
\end{itemize}

From an implementation lens, the practical implication is straightforward: if the objective is to improve net terminal wealth and deliver a robust investor experience, prioritizing a cost-aware rebalance policy and reducing unnecessary trading is likely to be more impactful than swapping among closely related dispersion measures inside an otherwise unchanged optimizer. Robust dispersion proxies remain useful---particularly as guardrails against outlier-driven tightening of risk constraints---but the results suggest that turnover-aware design is the more direct lever for economic viability in this setting.

